**Corollary 6.3 (no uniformly sublinear error for arbitrary completion).**
Fix $r\ge1$ and $d\ge2$, and choose one $m\ge2$ with $4m>r$.
For the fixed positive law (OCC.18), replacing its actual Gibbs law by
an arbitrary globally compatible completion that matches all COK local
laws and all full conditionals for at most $r$ owners cannot be
justified by a uniform additive $o(n)$ free-energy error. This remains
true with incidence at most four and terminal individual-bijection
witnesses.

Proof. Use Theorem 5.3 with this fixed $m$, $\epsilon=1/4$, and
$\ell\to\infty$. All those data match exactly, but the deficit is
at least $3\ell/4-7/4$ on $n=11m\ell$ blocks. Its liminf per
original block is at least $3/(44m)>0$. The actual witnesses have
the claimed incidence and terminality by Theorems 4.2 and 4.4.
$\square$

The connected family in §4 supplies the linear move barrier; the
extensive ambiguity in §5 uses disjoint copies of it. No assertion of
an extensive ambiguity in one connected growing gadget is needed.
Joint elimination, direct component summation, other global relations,
lawful finer recuts, global recoloring and unrestricted cap verifiers
remain available. [SCP][SCP]'s analytical soft carriers change the
support being summed and require their own estimates; they are not
counterexamples to this hard-support statement. This boundary neither
contradicts COK soundness nor proves noncomputability or failure of
unrestricted optimizer-cofinality.

